  &lt;div class="metric"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Squared Euclidean distance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong id="euclideanSquared"&gt;—&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Variables&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong id="dimension"&gt;—&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;div class="metric"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Matrix condition estimate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong id="condition"&gt;—&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id="interpretation" class="status"&gt;Enter a target, mean, and covariance matrix, then calculate.&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div class="result-section"&gt;
  &lt;h3&gt;Difference vector (x − μ)&lt;/h3&gt;
  &lt;div id="delta" class="muted"&gt;—&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;h2&gt;What is being calculated?&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mahalanobis distance measures how far a vector is from a reference mean while accounting for covariance among variables.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Squared Mahalanobis distance: D² = (x − μ)ᵀ P (x − μ)&lt;/strong&gt;, where P is the precision matrix (Σ⁻¹).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Squared Euclidean distance: E² = Σᵢ(xᵢ − μᵢ)²&lt;/strong&gt;; Euclidean distance is √E².&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="muted"&gt;Euclidean distance treats each variable axis equally in the supplied units. Mahalanobis distance accounts for covariance and variable scales. Neither distance is a probability by itself.&lt;/p&gt;





